# <type>(<catalog>): <subject>
#
# Conventional Commits, in English.
#
# SCOPE is the catalog the change touches, e.g. feat(core): ...
# OMIT the scope when the change belongs to no catalog — the harness, CI,
# docs, or tooling: docs: ...
# Legal scopes are the catalogs listed in ARCHITECTURE.md.
#
# TYPE is chosen by effect on installed behavior, not by file format:
#   feat     adds a capability a target project gets
#   fix      corrects wrong, misleading, over-restrictive, or over-permissive
#            installed behavior
#   refactor restructures without changing installed behavior
#   docs     supporting docs only; changes no installed skill
#   test     validators and their self-tests
#   ci       workflows and automation
#   chore    tooling and housekeeping
#
# Markdown is a file format, not a reason to use `docs`.
# Fix before feat: correcting existing behavior wins over the fact that the
# result is also an improvement.
#
# BODY: explain why, not what. Wrap at 72 characters.
#
# Before committing: run `just check`, and read the staged diff yourself for
# secrets and personal data. A secret committed is leaked even if a later
# commit removes it. Never use --no-verify.
